    "question": {
      ":question/id": "64187e16-377f-538d-9ae9-7548e89ead9e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $x^2-15x+\\frac{117}{2}$.\n\nFill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$x^2-15x+\\frac{117}{2}=(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^2+$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-15x+\\frac{117}{2}&=\\underbrace{x^2-2\\cdot \\frac{15}{2}x+\\frac{225}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{225}{4}+\\frac{117}{2} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{225}{4}+\\frac{117}{2} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{9}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-15x$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{15}{2}$ and $\\frac{9}{4}$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
